| The WhatsApp server (#1352): one WhatsApp Business number on Meta's Cloud API (Go's `net/http`), its messages by Meta's webhook, which the server serves on `WHATSAPP_WEBHOOK_ADDR` behind a tunnel or reverse proxy, each call checked with the app secret's signature: `message.received` (filters `space`, the person's number, `text`, `direct`; with its text) and `question.answered` (filters `space`, `question`), each with its `sender` (the number and the profile's name) for a channel link, #1762; `send_message`, `ask_question` (reply buttons, or a list for more than three options; the first press counts), `send_template` (for more than 24 hours after the person last wrote), and the messaging contract's `list_conversations`, `read_messages` (#4341: a person's messages both ways, newest first, by cursor, marked as outside content, from a bounded store in `~/.agents/whatsapp/`, the newest 2,000 for 30 days) and `reply` (#4345: the person's reply from the Messages page, as the number); `WHATSAPP_TOKEN`, `WHATSAPP_PHONE_NUMBER_ID`, `WHATSAPP_APP_SECRET` and `WHATSAPP_VERIFY_TOKEN` in the secrets; Agent Loops on the Mac carries it, every Linux host installs it, and the catalogue lists it as `agents/whatsapp` in the Agent Loops marketplace | agents | built | Tools, Events | 2026-11-04, against a fake Cloud API and signed webhook calls only (`Go/internal/whatsappmcp`) | `Go/cmd/whatsapp-mcp/main.go`, `Go/internal/whatsappmcp` | a real Meta app and test number (`needs-account`, Alex's test); the public address is the person's to give (a tunnel or a reverse proxy) until the control plane relays webhooks (FR-036) |
